  \begin{longtable}[]{@{}cccccc@{}}
  \toprule\noalign{}
  \(\text{W}\) & \(0\) & \(3\) & \(5\) & \(10\) & \(15\) \\
  \midrule\noalign{}
  \endhead
  \bottomrule\noalign{}
  \endlastfoot
  \(\text{P(W=w)}\) & \(0.16\) & \(0.03\) & \(0\) & \(0.39\) &
  \(0.18\) \\
  \end{longtable}

  \begin{enumerate}
  \def\labelenumii{(\alph{enumii})}
  \item
    \(79.47\)
  \item
    \(86.46\)
  \item
    \(73.08\)
  \item
    \(35.01\)
  \item
    \(124.53\)
  \end{enumerate}

  \textbf{Solution}\\
  Para o cálculo da variância precisamos, antes, calcular os valores de
  \(E(W)\) e \(E(W^2)\): \[\begin{aligned}
  E(W) &= \sum_{w} w~P(W=w) \\
  & = 0 \times 0.16+
  3 \times 0.03+5 \times 0+
  10 \times 0.39+15 \times 0.18 = 6.69, 
  \end{aligned}\] \[\begin{aligned}
  E(W^2) &= \sum_{w} w^2~P(W=w) \\
  & = 0 \times 0.16+
  9 \times 0.03+25 \times 0+
  100 \times 0.39+225 \times 0.18 \\
  & =79.77.
  \end{aligned}\] De modo que
  \(V(W) = E(W^2) - \left\{E(W)\right\}^2 = 35.01\).

